{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nResidual advisory findings from the Gerrit `LLM-Review` bot on change 2256 (the\n`e668-b496-e264-4283` mutation sandbox). None blocked the merge; each is a real but\nlower-severity gap. The two higher-severity findings from the same review are tracked\nseparately as `724c-b5fd-efb1-40d5` and `c100-c4f1-2a2b-4905`.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **Docs contradict the code on the venv.** `docs/local-dev-env.md` describes the\n   sandbox as permitting writes to \"the venv\", but the merged code deliberately\n   EXCLUDES the venv from the allow-list so a mutant cannot write executable code into\n   site-packages. The doc describes a weaker sandbox than the one that shipped; a\n   reader trusting it would mis-model the boundary.\n\n2. **A missing allow path is skipped silently.** `mutation_sandbox` skips a\n   non-existent allow-list path with a bare `continue` rather than surfacing it, because\n   `bwrap --bind` requires SRC to exist. A required path that a future caller fails to\n   create becomes an invisible loss of a writable bind — indistinguishable from an\n   intentionally absent one, and surfacing later as a confusing write failure inside the\n   sandbox.\n\n3. **The CI fail-open keys on an ambient, trivially-set variable** (observability\n   only here; the gate itself is tracked separately — see the criterion). When no mechanism is\n   available and `CI` is truthy, the run proceeds UNSANDBOXED with a warning; on a\n   workstation it aborts. `CI` is inheritable and can be set by anything, so a\n   workstation with `CI=1` exported silently runs mutation tests unsandboxed. The\n   cross-provider `CI` convention is the right portability choice (naming no provider),\n   but it should not be the SOLE gate on the fail-open.\n\n4. **The Seatbelt profile restricts writes only.** `(allow default)` + `(deny\n   file-write*)` leaves reads and network unrestricted, so a mutant under the macOS\n   sandbox can still exfiltrate or read anything the user can. Write-denial was the\n   incident's hazard and is the ticket's scope, but the residual read/network surface\n   should be a recorded decision rather than an accident.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `docs/local-dev-env.md` matches the shipped allow-list: the venv is NOT writable\n      inside the sandbox. Verified against `scripts/mutation_gate.py`, where the\n      allow-list is actually built — `execute_shard` sets `sandbox_allow = (root,\n      basetemp)` with `sys.prefix` deliberately absent. `mutation_sandbox.wrap()` only\n      RECEIVES `allow` as a parameter and constructs nothing.\n- [ ] A missing allow-list path is surfaced (log at WARNING naming the path) rather than\n      skipped silently. Verified by a test asserting the warning names the path.\n- [ ] The fail-open's WARNING makes the condition unmistakable in the log of any run\n      that took it: it states the run is UNSANDBOXED and names both the variable that\n      permitted it and its observed value. Verified by asserting that text.\n\n      SCOPE, stated so it is not mistaken for the stronger claim: this criterion improves\n      OBSERVABILITY only. It does NOT make the fail-open require more than ambient `CI`,\n      and after this ticket ambient `CI` remains the sole condition. Tightening that gate\n      is a behaviour change affecting every lane that relies on the fallback, so it needs\n      its own review rather than riding along here; tracked as `f11d-f8fd`.\n- [ ] The Seatbelt profile's read/network posture is a stated, deliberate decision\n      recorded in `docs/mutation-testing.md`, not an unremarked default.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- Each finding above is either fixed or recorded as a deliberate decision with reasoning.\n- `make lint`, `make typecheck`.\n\n## Notes\n\nDiscovered from `e668-b496-e264-4283`. Source: change 2256 review, patch sets 3-7."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787893049024164001,"uuid":"b5ba739e-e239-4018-880d-4e416e1447d5"}